# FACTORY (the author, 2026-10-05): `@: h f(a)` receives a factory's result; this row wrote `h: f a` until then.
# A returned nested definition gives a merge as the actual of a callable
# formal. inner reads maker's n through its captured occurrence. Its native
# implementation and its interpreted body must both construct the merge
# and preserve the caller's hidden inputs. five reads other, a field of
# the unit, 9. g holds maker 100: (g: 5) is 100 + 5 + 9 and (g: 7) is 116.
# under has other 20: 125. The merge is written with the model first and
# the bound formal is left at its default: a bounded case
# (steps/defects.md, MERGE-KEEPS-MODEL-INTERFACE). Entry 7.
int: other 9

fn: five (int: y) int
    return: y + other

fn: bin () int
    return: 0

fn: take (bin: op) int
    return: op()

fn: maker (int: n) fn: (int: q) int
    fn: inner (int: q) int
        return: n + (take: merge(five; y: q))
    return: inner

@: g maker(100)

fn: under () int
    int: other 20
    return: (g: 5)
end: under

int: a (g: 5)
int: b (g: 7)
int: c under()
int: ok 7
if: a != 114 || b != 116 || c != 125
    ok: 81
sendMessage: exit(exit_code: ok; stdout: ""; stderr: "")
return
